---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro';
import Analogy from '../../components/Analogy.astro';
import QuoteEstimator from '../../components/QuoteEstimator.astro';
import { RATES, QUALITIES } from '../../lib/estimate.ts';
import { materialById } from '../../data/materials.ts';
const pla = materialById('pla')!;
const std = QUALITIES.find((q) => q.id === 'standard')!;
---

<Guide slug="pricing">
  <h2>Why it matters</h2>
  <p>
    People are often surprised that a palm-sized print costs more than the plastic in it. A 70 g figurine uses about
    $1.40 of filament, yet it ties up a printer for over four hours. Knowing where the cost goes helps you ask for the
    right thing: a hollower part, a coarser layer, or a smaller size can halve the price.
  </p>

  <h2>The intuition</h2>
  <Analogy title="A loaf of bread with a crust">
    <p>
      A 3D print is mostly <strong>crust</strong> and <strong>crumb</strong>. The outside is a solid skin a few lines
      thick; the inside is a light honeycomb, the <em>infill</em>. Doubling a part's size makes the crumb grow much
      faster than the crust, so big prints are mostly infill, and the infill setting matters most for them.
    </p>
  </Analogy>

  <figure class="panel xs">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 560 200" role="img" aria-label="Cross-section of a print showing skin and infill">
      <desc>
        Cross-sections of a small and a large printed cube. Each has a solid orange skin of the same thickness and a
        sparse grid of infill inside. In the larger cube the skin is a much smaller fraction of the material.
      </desc>
      <defs>
        <pattern id="infill" width="14" height="14" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse" patternTransform="rotate(45)">
          <path d="M0 0H14M0 0V14" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5"></path>
        </pattern>
      </defs>
      <g>
        <rect x="40" y="70" width="90" height="90" fill="var(--accent)" rx="2"></rect>
        <rect x="52" y="82" width="66" height="66" fill="var(--bg-2)"></rect>
        <rect x="52" y="82" width="66" height="66" fill="url(#infill)"></rect>
        <text x="85" y="186" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">Small: skin ≈ half the plastic</text>
      </g>
      <g>
        <rect x="260" y="20" width="180" height="140" fill="var(--accent)" rx="2"></rect>
        <rect x="272" y="32" width="156" height="116" fill="var(--bg-2)"></rect>
        <rect x="272" y="32" width="156" height="116" fill="url(#infill)"></rect>
        <text x="350" y="186" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">Large: mostly infill</text>
      </g>
      <line x1="440" y1="40" x2="470" y2="40" stroke="var(--text-2)"></line>
      <text x="474" y="44" class="lbl">skin {RATES.wallMm} mm</text>
      <line x1="428" y1="100" x2="470" y2="100" stroke="var(--text-2)"></line>
      <text x="474" y="104" class="lbl">infill</text>
    </svg>
    <figcaption class="hint">Schematic, not to scale.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>The price then has four parts:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Filament</strong>: grams × the spool price (PLA ≈ ${pla.pricePerKg}/kg).</li>
    <li><strong>Machine time</strong>: hours × ${RATES.machinePerHour.toFixed(2)}/h for electricity, wear and maintenance. Usually the biggest slice.</li>
    <li><strong>Handling</strong>: ${RATES.handling.toFixed(2)} per job for slicing, removing supports, cleanup and packing.</li>
    <li><strong>Margin</strong>: {RATES.margin * 100}% on top, which pays for failed prints, design time and keeping the lab running.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Try it</h2>
  <div class="wide"><QuoteEstimator /></div>

  <h2>Worked example: an 8 × 8 × 10 cm figurine</h2>
  <p>These are the estimator's default inputs, solved by hand (the site's tests check the same numbers):</p>
  <div class="math">{`Object volume   8 × 8 × 10 cm × 0.35 (organic shape)      = 224 cm³
Skin            6 × 224^(2/3) cm² × 0.12 cm               = 26.6 cm³
Infill          15% × (224 − 26.6) cm³                    = 29.6 cm³
Plastic         26.6 + 29.6 = 56.2 cm³ × ${pla.density} g/cm³        = 69.7 g
Print time      69.7 g ÷ ${std.gramsPerHour} g/h (standard, 0.20 mm)        = 4 h 39 min

Filament        0.0697 kg × $${pla.pricePerKg}/kg                        = $1.39
Machine         4.64 h × $${RATES.machinePerHour.toFixed(2)}/h                            = $4.64
Handling                                                  = $3.00
Margin          ${RATES.margin * 100}% × $9.03                                = $3.61
Price           $12.65 → rounded up                       = $13`}</div>
  <p>
    Notice the filament is only about a tenth of the price. Dropping to draft layers cuts the machine time by about
    40%. Raising infill to 50% adds about 50 g of plastic, and the hours to print it.
  </p>

  <h2>Where this stops</h2>
  <div class="stops">
    <ul>
      <li><strong>It's a ballpark (±30%).</strong> The real number comes from slicing the actual model. Supports, overhangs and colour changes can add a lot.</li>
      <li><strong>Shape factor is a guess.</strong> The estimator doesn't see your model. A hollow vase or a dense brick can be far off.</li>
      <li><strong>Design work isn't included.</strong> Custom figurines from photos include modelling time and are quoted separately.</li>
      <li><strong>Multi-colour prints</strong> waste filament purging between colours and take longer. Ask for a quote.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</Guide>

<style>
  .xs {
    margin: 20px 0;
    padding: 16px;
  }
  .xs svg {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }
  .lbl {
    font: 500 11px var(--font);
    fill: var(--text-2);
  }
  figcaption {
    margin-top: 6px;
  }
</style>
